---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import GifAudioToMp3 from '@/components/tools/media/GifAudioToMp3.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="aac-to-mp3">
  <GifAudioToMp3
    client:load
    accept=".aac,.m4a,.mp4,.adts,.aacp,audio/aac,audio/mp4"
    sourceLabel="AAC"
    sourceHint="Raw .aac streams, ADTS captures and .m4a files all decode here"
    target="mp3"
    defaultBitrate="256"
    formatNote="Raw AAC has no container, so it carries no tags and no reliable duration. This is normal. The conversion works, and the progress bar uses FFmpeg's own reports."
  />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>Raw AAC versus M4A</h2>
    <p>
      Both hold the same codec. The difference is the packaging.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th></th><th>.aac (raw ADTS)</th><th>.m4a (MPEG-4)</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Structure</td><td>A bare sequence of frames, each with its own small header</td><td>A container with an index, a sample table and metadata atoms</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Tags</td><td>None</td><td>Title, artist, album, artwork, chapters</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Seeking</td><td>Approximate: players estimate from bitrate</td><td>Exact, from the index</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Typical source</td><td>Broadcast and stream captures, HLS segments, embedded devices</td><td>iTunes, Voice Memos, phone recorders</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Robustness</td><td>Can be cut anywhere and still mostly play</td><td>A truncated file may not open at all</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      This tool accepts both. For an Apple export, the <a href="/m4a-to-mp3">M4A to MP3</a> page has
      more on Voice Memos, GarageBand and DRM.
    </p>

    <h2>AAC is the better codec. Why convert down?</h2>
    <p>
      This conversion gives no gain in quality. AAC was designed as the successor to MP3 and is better
      at every bitrate, most of all below 128 kbps. All reasons to convert are about compatibility:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Car head units and factory stereos, mostly from before about 2015.</li>
      <li>DJ software and controllers with fixed format support.</li>
      <li>Cheap MP3 players, gym equipment, in-flight entertainment, hotel clock radios.</li>
      <li>Voicemail systems, telephony platforms and IVR uploads that require MP3.</li>
      <li>Older transcription and audio-analysis tools with narrow decoders.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      If all your devices read AAC, conversion only makes the file bigger and a little worse. Keep the
      original.
    </p>

    <h2>Choosing a bitrate for a second-generation encode</h2>
    <p>
      The source is already lossy, so the artefacts of the two encoders add up. AAC and MP3 discard
      different parts of the signal. The damage shows where AAC kept detail that MP3 handles poorly:
      pre-echo on sharp transients and high-frequency detail on cymbals.
    </p>
    <p>
      Encode at or above the source bitrate. The default here is 256 kbps: iTunes-era AAC is usually
      256 kbps, and most broadcast captures are between 96 and 192 kbps. Use 128 kbps only for speech.
    </p>
    <p>
      Do not chain conversions. AAC to MP3 to AAC to MP3 is four lossy passes, and by the fourth the
      cymbals sound like frying bacon. Always start from the earliest copy you have.
    </p>

    <h2>When a raw .aac file will not open anywhere</h2>
    <p>
      This is common, and the cause is usually the file. Raw ADTS streams captured mid-broadcast often
      start partway through a frame or end abruptly. If FFmpeg reports invalid data here, other tools
      read the same broken bytes and fail the same way. Download or capture the file again.
    </p>
    <p>
      Some files with an <code>.aac</code> extension are HE-AAC v2 with spectral band replication, and a
      few decoders handle the parametric stereo layer badly. FFmpeg decodes it correctly, but the output
      can be mono where you expected stereo. This comes from the source.
    </p>

    <h2>Batch conversion, no queue, no cap</h2>
    <p>
      Up to fifty files convert one after another, each with its own progress row, and the set
      downloads as a ZIP. Nothing is uploaded: the AAC decoder and the LAME MP3 encoder are part of the
      WebAssembly build of FFmpeg that runs in this tab. There is no daily limit, and the only size
      limit is your device's memory.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
